    "description": "IroncladBuffer is a reusable C++ storage class whose design currently combines several independent decisions: ownership of memory, bounds-check strength, allocation behavior, and whether internal access is serialized. Development builds need exhaustive checks, production builds need low overhead, embedded deployments need a fixed arena, and threaded deployments need locking. Each combination must remain type-safe, inlineable, and fixed before execution begins; a configuration read in a constructor is not acceptable because changing it would alter the class's structure or thread contract. Users should be able to instantiate only coherent combinations and should see incompatible combinations as compile errors. The buffer also needs to expose a stable public API and support value semantics where appropriate. The implementation must avoid copying a single implementation into several specialized classes and must not add a virtual layer to each buffer operation merely to select behavior. The decision is where the ownership, checking, allocation, and synchronization variations should be bound and selected during type construction.",
    "decision_statement": "Decide how IroncladBuffer should bind its independent ownership, checking, allocation, and synchronization decisions at compile time so each instantiation is fixed, type-safe, and inlineable.",
    "notes": "Policy-based-design fits the orthogonal ownership, checking, allocation and synchronization decisions bound at instantiation; traits, crtp and tag-dispatching are near-misses (external descriptors, mixin bases, per-property selection) and envelope-letter supports the stable public API."
  },
